#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# numerical_sort

#% text
# `numerical_sort(xs)` sorts the elements of `xs` (a list, or any compound term, whose head stays) in the order of `numerical_order`: numbers (`inf` and `-inf` included, complex numbers by real part, then size of imaginary part) by value; then quantities, by value where the units are compatible and by unit otherwise (lengths before times); then compound terms, by head, then shorter first, then argument by argument numerically; then the other terms in canonical order. `sort` uses the canonical order instead, which does not order numerical constants by value.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
numerical_sort([1, pi, e, inf, -sqrt(2), -inf])
#% output
#= [-inf, -2^(1/2), 1, e, pi, inf]

#% cell
numerical_sort(data(0, 1, inf, -inf, x))
#% output
#= data(-inf, 0, 1, inf, x)

#% cell
sort([1, pi, e, inf, -sqrt(2), -inf])
#% output
#= [1, e, inf, pi, -inf, -2^(1/2)]

#% cell
numerical_sort([b, a, inf, 0])
#% output
#= [0, inf, a, b]

#% text
# Compound terms by their arguments (the shorter first), quantities by unit and then by value:

#% cell
numerical_sort([h(0, 2, foo), h(inf, 1), h(-inf, 3)])
#% output
#= [h(-inf, 3), h(inf, 1), h(0, 2, foo)]

#% cell
numerical_sort([quantity(1, "Yards"), quantity(1, "Minutes"), quantity(50, "Centimeters"), quantity(30, "Seconds")])
#% output
#= [quantity(50, "Centimeters"), quantity(1, "Yards"), quantity(30, "Seconds"), quantity(1, "Minutes")]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `sort`, `sort_by`, `ordering`, `numerical_order`
